
/*
 * Creates a new machine learning type, which can later be populated with RAG data.
 */
.arguments

   // Injected by the framework.
   _session:string

   // Mandatory name of type.
   type:string

   // Optional system instruction for the machine learning type.
   system_message:string

   // Optional comma separated list of roles. The user must belong to at least one of these roles to be able to use machine learning type.
   auth:string

   // Optional maximum context tokens, implying how many tokens of RAG data will be extracted on each request towards the LLM.
   max_context_tokens:int

   // Optional boolean indicating whether or not the type will turn on or off automatic embeddings.
   use_embeddings:bool

// Ensuring user is root.
auth.ticket.verify:root

// Sanity checking invocation
validators.mandatory:x:@.arguments/*/type
validators.regex:x:@.arguments/*/type
   regex:^[a-z0-9_-]+$

// Applying defaults.
validators.default:x:@.arguments
   model:gpt-5-chat-latest
   max_tokens:int:12000
   max_context_tokens:int:12000
   max_request_tokens:int:12000
   max_function_invocations:int:100
   temperature:decimal:0.3
   supervised:int:1
   use_embeddings:int:1
   cached:int:0
   threshold:decimal:0.3
   greeting:Hi there, I am your virtual AI-based assistant. How can I help you?
   system_message:
   conversation_starters:@"* What can I ask you about?
* Who created you?"

// Ensuring use_embeddings is an integer.
if
   or
      eq:x:@.arguments/*/use_embeddings
         .:bool:true
      eq:x:@.arguments/*/use_embeddings
         .:int:1
   .lambda
      set-value:x:@.arguments/*/use_embeddings
         .:int:1
else
   set-value:x:@.arguments/*/use_embeddings
      .:int:0

// Default system instruction
.instruction:@"## General instructions

* Always end your response with a `FUNCTION_INVOCATION` when executing functions, and return the `FUNCTION_INVOCATION` parts in the same message as the message you intend to execute the function in.
* Never execute a function unless you know its exact filename and signature.
* When executing one or more functions in the current response, no text is allowed after the final `FUNCTION_INVOCATION` block.
* If a function does not return anything then inform the user that the function didn't return anything.
* If the user asks you what you can do then use the `list-functions` function and group its result into categories explaining each individual function and workflow.
* Today's date and time is {{
date.now
date.format:x:-
   format:""yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ""
return:x:-
}} UTC

## Image instructions

* If you find relevant images in the context then return these images as follows to the user ![image_description](image_url).
* ONLY display images you find in the context.
* If you cannot find an image in the context then DO NOT MAKE UP IMAGE URLS.

## Functions

You can execute functions by ending your response with something resembling the following:

```plaintext
___
FUNCTION_INVOCATION[/FOLDER/FILENAME.hl]:
{
  ""arg1"": ""value1"",
  ""arg2"": 1234
}
___
```

The above is only an example and not a real function.

`FUNCTION_INVOCATION` blocks must contain valid JSON arguments matching the function signature. The wrapper syntax (`___` and `FUNCTION_INVOCATION[...]`) is transport metadata and is not itself JSON.

### Function execution instructions

* Never execute a function before you've got all the mandatory arguments or confirmed that defaults are acceptable.
* If you are about to execute a function, end your response with the invocation in the same message.
* Wrap the entire `FUNCTION_INVOCATION` header and JSON payload inside a single block bounded by `___` at the top and bottom.
* Each argument can only be supplied once.
* Unless you know an argument's value, omit it entirely.
* You may return multiple `FUNCTION_INVOCATION` blocks in the same response only when later invocations do not depend on earlier return values, side effects, or created resources.
* If you experience repeated execution errors, stop and ask the user for help."

if
   eq:x:@.arguments/*/use_embeddings
      .:bool:true
   .lambda
      set-value:x:@.instruction
         strings.concat
            get-value:x:@.instruction
            .:"\n\n"
            .:@"## Adhere to the context

* YOU ARE UNDER NO CIRCUMSTANCES ALLOWED TO ANSWER QUESTIONS YOU CANNOT FIND THE ANSWER TO IN THE CONTEXT.
* If the user asks you a question and you cannot find the answer to it in the context, or the question is irrelevant to the provided context, then inform the user that you don't know the answer, and encourage the user to provide some relevant keywords or stay on subject."

else
   set-value:x:@.instruction
      strings.concat
         get-value:x:@.instruction
         .:"\n\n"
         .:@"Below you can find some functions you can use to your benefit.

### List all functions

If the user asks you to list all functions or what you can do for them or help them with, then you must end your response with the following.

```plaintext
___
FUNCTION_INVOCATION[/system/misc/workflows/list-functions.hl]
___
```

This function will return RAG functions which are dynamically looked up using VSS based upon the specified prompt, in addition to system message functions which are always a part of the context allowing you to always execute these without a prompt matching based upon VSS.

If you need to execute one of these functions and you don't know its exact FUNCTION_INVOCATION declaration, you can use the `get-context` function to retrieve its exact declaration and how to correctly parametrize and execute it.

### Search for function, workflow, or information (`get-context`)

If the user is asking you to search for a function, or you cannot find the required function or information required to perform some task, then use the following function to search for additional information, and/or functions, and/or workflows:

```plaintext
___
FUNCTION_INVOCATION[/misc/workflows/workflows/misc/get-context.hl]:
{
  ""query"": ""[QUERY]"",
  ""max_tokens"": 5000
}
___
```

The above can have a [QUERY] value being for instance ""Create module"", ""Delete file"", ""Workflow for how to create an AI chatbot embed script"", or ""Create CRUD API"", etc. The `max_tokens` argument is how many tokens to return. 5000 is a good number here, but if you cannot find anything, you can increase `max_tokens`, vary your prompt, and try again.

The `get-context` function will return RAG records using VSS, and might return irrelevant information due to it searching using VSS. Records are returned as a string where each individual record is separated by a `---` line, and each new function, workflow, or information snippet starts with a markdown H1 title.

If the user’s query is broad (e.g., “list companies,” “show users,” “find projects”) and you don’t know a direct function, call `get-context` with the query as-is to look for relevant workflows, information, or functions."

// Making sure we add support for executing AI functions to the system instruction.
set-value:x:@.arguments/*/system_message
   strings.concat
      get-value:x:@.arguments/*/system_message
      .:"\n\n"
      .get-value:x:@.instruction

// Trimming.
set-value:x:@.arguments/*/system_message
   strings.trim:x:@.arguments/*/system_message

// Storing session since we're for simplicity reasons getting rid of it.
.session
set-value:x:-
   get-value:x:@.arguments/*/_session
remove-nodes:x:@.arguments/*/_session

// Connecting to our database.
data.connect:magic

   // Parametrizing create invocation.
   add:x:./*/data.create/*/values
      get-nodes:x:@.arguments/*

   // Inserting our record
   data.create
      table:ml_types
      values

// Signaling frontend
sockets.signal:x:@.session
   args
      type:floating_message
      floating_message:Machine learning type successfully created
      floating_state:success

// Returning result to caller.
return
   result:success
   message:Notice, instructions for AI functions, handling images during the conversation, anti-AI hallucination, and follow up questions was automatically added to the instruction.
